WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.470
QUENTIN HARDY: I'm
here with Suzanne Frey,

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.620
director of security, trust,
and privacy at Google Cloud.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.585
She is responsible for ensuring
we gain our customers' trust

00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:21.160
with our extensive transparency,
visibility, and controls.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.660
Suzanne, thanks for taking
the time to be with us today.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.659
SUZANNE FREY: Thank you.

00:00:24.659 --> 00:00:27.220
QUENTIN HARDY: So why should
businesses trust Google Cloud?

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.430
What kind of commitments do
we make that protects them?

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.800
SUZANNE FREY: Google
was born in the cloud,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.180
and we've been
cloud-native since day one.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.590
Now, what does that mean?

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:40.150
That means that we've thought
about security and privacy

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:44.930
in how we build and architect
our data centers, servers,

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:48.670
networks, devices,
and applications.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.950
It's allowed us to move
at the speed of the world

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.097
since day one, with respect to
security and privacy threats.

00:00:54.097 --> 00:00:55.930
And I think that's super
important for folks

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:57.100
to understand.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.144
We have optimized for the cloud.

00:00:59.144 --> 00:01:01.060
QUENTIN HARDY: And how
does that get reflected

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.590
in the commitments we make?

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:03.631
SUZANNE FREY: Absolutely.

00:01:03.631 --> 00:01:06.880
So we put our money
where our mouth is.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.670
We put all of our commitments
to security and privacy

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.020
in contract.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.910
These are binding
documents, as you know,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.220
so that customers
can feel comfortable

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:20.270
knowing that we are committed
to their security and privacy.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.780
QUENTIN HARDY: Now, the
customers still know us

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.600
very much as a
consumer company--

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.990
strong brand, very
strong consumer presence.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.820
And enterprise
customers sometimes

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.800
have a few questions
around that.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.020
Can you give me some
clarity on the differences,

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.950
and what exactly happens to
enterprise data, for example?

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.800
SUZANNE FREY: So going back
to the cloud-native approach

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.280
that we've taken, we've
built a tremendous business.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.810
In fact, there's
seven businesses

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:51.730
at Google with over a billion
consumer users apiece.

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.250
And just look at that
track record, standalone.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.680
We bring all of the power
of that track record

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.910
to our enterprise
customers, and even more.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:04.090
We talked about
contracts, but we also

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.540
commit to not scanning
your data for advertising.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.479
We commit that you can
take your data with you.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.430
We commit that you have
a right to understand

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.474
where your data is being stored,
and your IP will belong to you.

00:02:16.474 --> 00:02:17.890
QUENTIN HARDY: And
earlier, when I

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.250
was talking with Dan and Rene,
we talked about transparency,

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.790
because cloud relationships
aren't like standard packaged

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.110
enterprise software
relationships.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:28.910
We don't sell and leave.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.440
We have to earn it and win
it on this rolling basis,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.000
and also keep people informed
around new features, what's

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.010
coming up on the product side.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.730
And they were saying that
the change management

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.080
and the training involved makes
transparency very important,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.760
and communications important.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.420
From Google's perspective,
why is transparency important?

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.920
SUZANNE FREY: Well,
you put yourself

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.430
in the shoes of any CIO or
CSO who is making a decision

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.850
to move from on-premise to the
cloud, or even another cloud

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.580
provider to ours,
and you really have

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:09.070
to understand, how am I going
to know I am safe and secure?

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.730
We believe we earn trust
through transparency.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.930
Knowing what's happening to
your data at any given time,

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.190
super critical for
earning that trust.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.690
QUENTIN HARDY:
Security is the sense

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.270
that you control your
environment, right?

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.270
And the transparency means
you can see and control

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.440
as much as possible.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.980
That's the idea
behind that, I guess.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.140
So finally, what
are three things

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.232
you'd like viewers to
take from this session?

00:03:34.232 --> 00:03:36.190
SUZANNE FREY: Well, we've
touched on all three,

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:37.150
in some capacity.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.600
For example, pillar
number one is compliance.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.540
We're committed to our
customers' compliance, examples

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.320
being things like
GDPR and FedRAMP.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.480
We have to be ahead of
the curve with respect

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.030
to global compliance
regimes, and we

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:55.660
recognize our compliance is
our customers' compliance.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.750
Two, we're committed
to transparency,

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.720
trust through transparency,
and enabling customers

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.480
to understand what we're
doing with their data.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.690
And thirdly, we're
committed to control.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:12.010
You are in control of
your data, and not only

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.130
with portability and
things like that, but also

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:19.570
the ability to turn
off services or control

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.740
the security of your devices,
or understand which executives

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.500
in your organization
should have a security key,

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.540
and implement and
enforce that at scale.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:30.990
So those are the three things--

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.899
compliance, transparency,
and control.

00:04:32.899 --> 00:04:34.940
QUENTIN HARDY: Suzanne,
thank you for joining us.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.040
SUZANNE FREY: Thank you.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

